WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.388
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.207 --> 00:00:09.790
ETSUJI NAKAI: I'm really happy
to see many people in this room

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:12.610
now, even though
this is the morning

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:14.000
session of the last day.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:14.500
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.500
And especially I see some
really enthusiastic people

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.940
in the front row.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.920
And in this session,
I will talk,

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.430
a little bit, practical aspects
of machine learning projects.

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:28.010
So if you are actually working
on some real machine learning

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:30.880
projects, I hope you
will find something

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.890
useful for your project
in this session.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.980
OK, anyway, I'm Etsuji Nakai.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.800
I'm a Solutions Architect
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.230
I'm based in Tokyo.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:44.500
And in this session, I will talk
about the continuous deployment

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:45.860
in machine learning.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.930
So what does it mean?

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.110
The concept of
continuous deployment

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:52.690
is fairly common
today, especially

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.280
in the field of
web applications.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:58.930
So for comparison, let's think
about the very traditional

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.930
enterprise business
application project.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:04.750
In that case, once
you have successfully

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:09.310
launched your initial version
of your business application,

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:12.220
it is basically the
end of the story.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.660
It's really a happy
ending of your project.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:20.290
However, if this is a project
of the modern web application,

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:22.360
especially for
consumer users, you

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:26.470
know the situation is different,
because the requirements

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.830
from end users, especially
from consumer users,

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:31.820
are always changing.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:35.180
So to keep up with
the changing reality,

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:39.050
you have to keep upgrading
your application.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.100
Sometimes you have to introduce
new technology to change

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.410
your application completely.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:47.715
So basically you have to
keep deploying new versions

00:01:47.715 --> 00:01:50.450
of your application forever.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.640
This is a basic concept
of continuous deployment

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.730
in web applications.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:02:00.910
And I'll say the same
concept can be, or should be,

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:04.810
applied to machine
learning projects.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:08.650
So let me start
with explaining what

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:14.150
continuous deployment means
in machine learning projects.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:17.470
And this is a very common
imagination about machine

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.140
learning among many people.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.080
I don't think it's
you, [LAUGHS] but still

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.300
many people believe
that machine learning

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.330
is a magical
mathematical algorithm.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:31.840
You have some data, you put
those data into the algorithm,

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.100
then you have a magical result.
Of course it's illusion.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.270
[LAUGHS]

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:39.580
As you know, the real machine
learning project, especially

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:41.950
the end-to-end process
of machine learning

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.040
involves a lot of human effort.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.610
First of all, you have to
collect your training data

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.855
and organize them with
some big data too,

00:02:51.855 --> 00:02:55.900
like BigQuery or
Cloud Dataflow on GCP.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.170
And then you need to
employ data scientists

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:02.800
to build machine learning model
according to your training

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:03.760
data.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.820
And then you use a bunch
of computing resources,

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:11.020
like Cloud Machine
Learning Engine on GCP,

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.430
to train your machine
learning model.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:16.960
And finally, [LAUGHS] you
can deploy the trained model

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.585
to the real world
as an API service

00:03:19.585 --> 00:03:22.990
or as in the back-end side
with a real machine learning

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:24.880
applications.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:30.400
And the good news is that you
have a lot of useful tools

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.450
to support this end-to-end
machine learning process

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.150
on GCP.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:39.860
And I think you have heard of
these tools in other sessions.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:45.250
However, in this session I don't
talk about these tools at all.

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:49.060
[LAUGHS] The main
focus of this session

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:54.820
is about feedback, because
the goal of machine learning

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:58.520
is to predict something
in the real world.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.240
And as you know, the real
world is always changing.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.650
So you need to have some
feedback from the real world

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.990
to upgrade your
machine learning model,

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:12.010
to keep the freshness of
your machine learning model.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:19.089
Now, the question is how and
why feedback or the freshness

00:04:19.089 --> 00:04:22.760
is important in
machine learning.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:26.260
So let me use this
very simple example

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:32.050
to explain why freshness matters
in machine learning models.

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:33.850
This is a tutorial article.

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:38.380
The title is "Machine Learning
with Structured Data."

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:40.300
I published this
article this year,

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:45.490
so you can find this article
by searching with these titles.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:47.890
And in this article,
I explained how

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:52.540
you can build machine learning
model to predict babies weight.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:56.530
On the left side of the screen
using an example application,

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:59.845
you specify some attributes
of pregnant person,

00:04:59.845 --> 00:05:02.800
and by clicking
the predict button,

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:08.200
you can make a prediction about
the newborn baby's weight,

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.350
babies who will be born
in the near future,

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:15.400
like in 2018 or 2019.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:20.230
I think this model has
some realistic use case.

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:22.560
I believe some doctors can
use this machine learning

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.240
model for some medical purpose.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:31.680
But anyway, in this article,
I used the open public data

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.760
set called the Natality
Data Set to train

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:36.820
this machine learning model.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:40.360
It is available for
everyone on BigQuery.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:43.060
It is birth records
in the United States

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:49.550
from 1969 to 2008, and it
contains more than 100 million

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:50.050
records.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:54.000
[LAUGHS] So in a
sense, it is big data.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.130
It contains data for
nearly 40 years--

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:58.170
very good.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:02.440
But let's look at a
snippet of the SQL

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:06.730
to extract our trend
data from BigQuery.

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:09.400
As you can see,
the WHERE clause,

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:14.500
I didn't use the whole 40 years
data to train this model--

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:18.430
I used only data after 2001.

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:22.330
So what's the problem with
using the whole data to train

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:24.730
this machine learning model?

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:28.820
And the answer is
data freshness,

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:31.670
because the goal
of this model is,

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:35.600
as I told you, to predict
a baby's weight who will be

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:39.380
born in near future, in 2018.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:43.520
So naturally thinking,
using fairly old data,

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:45.820
like in the '70s, to
train this machine

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:50.600
learning model may not
be good for that purpose,

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:54.350
because environments
surrounding people in the '70s

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:57.200
could be totally
different from today.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:00.600
So I did some experiment.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:03.020
First, I trained this
machine learning model

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:07.670
with the data in fairly
old days, like in the '70s.

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:12.800
And then I tested it against
the relatively new test data

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:14.780
in 2008.

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:18.730
And the result was
1.21 RMS error.

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:20.480
What does it mean?

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:23.240
It means the predictions
of this model

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:25.520
are different from
the ground truth,

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:31.830
different from of actual
value, in average, 1.21 pounds.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:34.140
Is it good or bad?

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:38.230
Of course, to understand
it, you have to compare.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:42.870
So then I trained the same
model with relatively new data

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:47.330
from 2001 to 2008
and again tested

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:50.820
with the same test set in 2008.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:55.500
And the result was
1.05 RMS error.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:08:00.850
So obviously, this result is
better than the previous one.

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:05.360
That's the reason why I used
data only after 2001 to train

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:07.090
this machine learning model.

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:07.590
Very good.

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:09.550
Make sense?

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:12.520
However, this is not
the end of the story.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:16.170
Now, suppose that I'm
really serious about using

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:18.810
this machine learning
model in the real world.

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:22.890
I want to provide this machine
learning model to real doctors.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:27.570
In that case, I have to validate
this machine learning model

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:33.690
against the latest
data, like in 2018.

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:37.650
Unfortunately, the
open Natality Data Set

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:43.590
did not have the data in
2018, so I couldn't test it.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:45.960
But naturally thinking,
because this model

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.940
was trained with
the data until 2008,

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:55.350
the test result
against 2018 test data

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:59.200
might be worse than
the previous one.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:03.600
So basically, if you are really
serious about using machine

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:08.310
learning model in the real
world for practical purposes,

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:12.660
you have to keep collecting
new data forever.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:15.330
And sometimes you have to
retrain your machine learning

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:18.810
model with fresh data to keep
the freshness of your machine

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:20.260
learning model.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:25.260
So this is the basic idea
of the continuous deployment

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:27.180
in machine learning.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:30.786
And the next
question is, how you

00:09:30.786 --> 00:09:34.590
can apply this concept in
your machine learning project.

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:37.815
What is the standard process
of continuous deployment

00:09:37.815 --> 00:09:39.180
in machine learning?

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:41.830
What is the best practice?

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:47.310
So to explain that today,
I prepared 1, 2, 3--

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:50.100
three user stories.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:54.360
And the first two stories
are just imaginary ones.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:58.200
You may think these are
really unnatural situations--

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:00.370
I admit it.

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:03.870
But at least I hope through
these imaginary stories

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:07.590
you will understand what is the
standard process of applying

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:09.800
continuous deployment
and what is

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:14.100
the value of continuous
deployment in machine learning.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:18.270
And the last one is
a real user story.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:23.340
Today I'm really happy to invite
[? Kazusa-san. ?] He's working

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:27.000
for BrainPad-- it is a machine
learning partner in Tokyo.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:30.800
He has a lot of experience of
managing real machine learning

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:31.710
projects.

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:34.290
He will talk about
his experience--

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:36.600
how and why
continuous deployment

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:39.870
is very important
in his projects, how

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:44.670
he applies continuous deployment
in his real projects-- later.

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:48.850
But anyway, let me start
with an imaginary story.

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:53.550
And for this story, I prepared
a fairly simple example

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:54.270
application.

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:57.120
It is a handwritten
digit recognizer.

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.520
You can draw some number
on the word canvas

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:02.070
and by clicking the
prediction button,

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:05.820
the model recognizes
what the number is--

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:07.240
very simple.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:11.040
And now suppose that there
is an imaginary company.

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:15.390
The CEO wants to release this
application to the real world

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:18.780
and make some money.

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:21.960
And as I told you, we have
two imaginary stories.

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:24.780
The first one is very bad.

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:27.820
They will make a silly mistake.

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:31.110
However, the important
thing is to understand

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:34.320
why such a silly
mistake had happened

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:38.320
and how they could have
avoided it by applying

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:41.250
the continuous deployment.

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:43.800
OK, so let's start
the first story.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:48.570
Please welcome the CEO of the
imaginary company, Nori-san

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:50.250
on the stage.

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:56.685
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:56.685 --> 00:11:59.159
[INAUDIBLE]

00:11:59.159 --> 00:12:01.200
NORIHIRO SHIMODA: Hi, nice
to meet you, everyone.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:02.510
I'm Nori.

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:04.970
So I've just started
my new company,

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:07.550
and I've created
a super fancy app

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:09.410
using a machine learning model.

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:11.520
But I have a problem.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:13.700
So I'm not sure how
much data we should

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:17.930
correct to train a machine
learning model appropriately.

00:12:17.930 --> 00:12:21.160
So my team had worked
very hard to correct

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:24.000
the initial set of
the training data,

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:27.440
but I still don't know
if it's enough or not.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:32.940
So I hired a funky wannabe
data scientist, so it's Yaboo.

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:34.490
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:37.100
So fortunately, he
had a brilliant idea

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:39.510
to evaluate our
machine learning model.

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:43.239
So Yaboo, please
tell me the details.

00:12:43.239 --> 00:12:44.280
YABOO OYABU: Hi everyone.

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:49.730
My name is Yaboo, and I'm a
funky wannabe data scientist.

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:54.380
So to make success in
machine learning project,

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:57.290
I read super-advanced
machine learning textbook.

00:12:57.290 --> 00:13:02.870
And according to it, you need to
split your data set to training

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:06.750
data set and test set.

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:07.610
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:09.330
It's very simple.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:11.840
The first, you should train
your machine learning model

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:14.540
with training data
set and by data model,

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:19.280
your test set so that your
machine learning model will not

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:22.700
[INAUDIBLE] to training data.

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:25.190
Actually, I've already
done it for you,

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:28.830
and I got perfect results.

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:30.330
We got 100% accuracy here.

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:31.520
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:34.415
This is super nice, right?

00:13:34.415 --> 00:13:35.060
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:39.030
And we should deploy this
machine learning model

00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:42.110
so as soon as possible
to our application.

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:44.984
So what do you think, Nori?

00:13:44.984 --> 00:13:46.150
NORIHIRO SHIMODA: Oh my god!

00:13:46.150 --> 00:13:47.440
I can't believe it!

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:48.330
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:50.171
So, nice work, Yaboo.

00:13:50.171 --> 00:13:51.960
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:55.160
I don't have any reason not
to release this application.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:57.324
OK, let's launch it.

00:13:57.324 --> 00:13:59.490
ETSUJI NAKAI: OK, thank
you, Nori-san and Yaboo-san.

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:00.780
You are good performers.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:04.021
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:10.290
Now, on the surface, they
did a fairly good job.

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:15.690
They achieved a brilliant
result, perfect accuracy.

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:18.790
And of course, they
made a mistake.

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:21.210
So let me show you
what would happen

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:25.005
if they launched this
application to the real world.

00:14:25.005 --> 00:14:26.905
Can you turn to the
demonstration screen?

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:32.950
Hello, can you turn
to the demonstration--

00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:34.130
OK.

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:37.880
This is the handwritten digit
recognizer that they launched.

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:39.560
So let me try.

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:42.410
I drew a number 0.

00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:44.920
Make a prediction.

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:47.200
OK, it's working.

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:48.220
Great.

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:52.522
Let me try number 1.

00:14:52.522 --> 00:14:53.500
It's strange.

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:59.560
[LAUGHS] Let me try number 2.

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:00.060
OK.

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:01.090
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:01.090 --> 00:15:03.580
It doesn't work.

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:08.560
So I tell you what had
happened under the covers.

