00:28:41.150 --> 00:28:49.200
And as you remember, perhaps,
he set the 90% accuracy

00:28:49.200 --> 00:28:50.940
as a threshold.

00:28:50.940 --> 00:28:54.250
It means they don't want to
use the machine learning model

00:28:54.250 --> 00:28:57.580
if the accuracy
is less than 90%.

00:28:57.580 --> 00:29:01.520
In that sense, they have
a problem on the last day,

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:03.510
day 10.

00:29:03.510 --> 00:29:10.060
The recovered accuracy on day
10 is slightly less than 90%.

00:29:10.060 --> 00:29:11.840
It's a problem for them.

00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:15.940
They have to do something to
recover from this situation.

00:29:15.940 --> 00:29:23.070
So let me explain first why this
slight decreasing had happened.

00:29:23.070 --> 00:29:26.140
It is the data complexity.

00:29:26.140 --> 00:29:29.680
As you know, every day they
keep collecting new data.

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:32.790
They have more and
more complex data.

00:29:32.790 --> 00:29:37.990
And if their initial
model was very simple,

00:29:37.990 --> 00:29:41.920
it could handle the very
simple first-day data.

00:29:41.920 --> 00:29:44.680
But it may be really
hard for the simple model

00:29:44.680 --> 00:29:48.790
to deal with very complex
data on the last day.

00:29:48.790 --> 00:29:51.490
So basically what
they have to do

00:29:51.490 --> 00:29:54.780
is just redesign their
machine learning model

00:29:54.780 --> 00:29:57.400
if they are using
neural network,

00:29:57.400 --> 00:30:01.200
they could add some more layers
to make the machine learning

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:06.520
model more complex to deal
with more complex data.

00:30:06.520 --> 00:30:09.670
So this is a
summary of the flow.

00:30:09.670 --> 00:30:13.730
On the top left, first
you build your machine

00:30:13.730 --> 00:30:16.990
learning model and
train with initial data.

00:30:16.990 --> 00:30:21.770
And you keep monitoring
every day, or every week,

00:30:21.770 --> 00:30:25.150
and if necessary, you retrain
your machine learning model.

00:30:25.150 --> 00:30:29.080
And sometimes you recover the
accuracy above the threshold.

00:30:29.080 --> 00:30:33.295
And sometimes you cannot
recover the accuracy even after

00:30:33.295 --> 00:30:35.140
the retraining.

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:37.660
In that case, it is
really good timing

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:41.820
for your project to redesign
your machine learning model.

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:45.280
You hire the real
data scientist again.

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:48.280
They will do some
investigation about the nature

00:30:48.280 --> 00:30:50.140
of the current data set.

00:30:50.140 --> 00:30:52.960
They will redesign the machine
learning model according

00:30:52.960 --> 00:30:55.890
to the current data set.

00:30:55.890 --> 00:30:59.190
OK, now I think you have
some basic idea what

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:02.670
is the process of the
continuous deployment.

00:31:02.670 --> 00:31:06.480
And finally, I will
invite Kazu-san

00:31:06.480 --> 00:31:09.750
from our machine learning
partner in Tokyo.

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:12.630
He will tell you
his real experience,

00:31:12.630 --> 00:31:17.490
how he applied the continuous
deployment in his real project.

00:31:17.490 --> 00:31:20.193
Please welcome
Kazu-san on the stage.

00:31:20.193 --> 00:31:23.574
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:29.156 --> 00:31:30.030
TAKETOSHI KAZUSA: Hi.

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:30.960
Good to see you.

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:36.420
I'm Kazu from BrainPad, which
is a GCP partner in Japan.

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:39.590
We have about 80 data
scientists in Tokyo.

00:31:39.590 --> 00:31:43.230
And we have developed
many, many systems

00:31:43.230 --> 00:31:50.450
in a wide variety of industries
for more than 10 years.

00:31:50.450 --> 00:31:53.100
One of our customer's
cases, Kewpie,

00:31:53.100 --> 00:31:56.920
was featured in
Google Cloud Next '17.

00:31:56.920 --> 00:31:59.900
Kewpie is a manufacturer
of food products.

00:31:59.900 --> 00:32:04.770
Their mayonnaise is very
popular in many countries.

00:32:04.770 --> 00:32:06.790
They operate machine
learning technology

00:32:06.790 --> 00:32:11.940
to detect bad potato cubes
in their production line.

00:32:11.940 --> 00:32:15.880
We implemented a data system
with a TensorFlow for them.

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:19.830
OK, let's turn back to the
main topic of this session.

00:32:19.830 --> 00:32:22.880
I'd like to talk about
another customer case

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:25.550
where we implemented the
continuous deployment

00:32:25.550 --> 00:32:27.510
for machine learning systems.

00:32:27.510 --> 00:32:30.450
I'd like to explain why the
continuous deployment is

00:32:30.450 --> 00:32:33.690
so important for ML models.

00:32:33.690 --> 00:32:37.830
Unfortunately, the data of this
customer case are confidential.

00:32:37.830 --> 00:32:42.360
So I'll tell some essential
aspects of [INAUDIBLE] what

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:45.770
I learned from this real case.

00:32:45.770 --> 00:32:48.780
The customer is logistics
service provider.

00:32:48.780 --> 00:32:50.930
They develop clients'
products [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:50.930 --> 00:32:53.100
their warehouses to
clients' outlets.

00:32:56.380 --> 00:33:00.650
So we help them to apply data
science and machine learning

00:33:00.650 --> 00:33:06.470
to optimize the delivery route
and minimize the delivery cost.

00:33:06.470 --> 00:33:08.490
Here's our solution.

00:33:08.490 --> 00:33:12.170
First, we implemented a machine
learning model [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:12.170 --> 00:33:14.810
as demand forecast
for each product

00:33:14.810 --> 00:33:18.910
using the data from
the CRM systems.

00:33:18.910 --> 00:33:21.590
And then we've
applied another model

00:33:21.590 --> 00:33:23.990
which calculates the best
delivery route, the best

00:33:23.990 --> 00:33:25.510
on this demand forecast.

00:33:28.310 --> 00:33:32.520
OK, so due to the
[INAUDIBLE] system,

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:36.830
our client saved about
$2 million per year.

00:33:36.830 --> 00:33:41.320
So let's focus on the continuous
deployment of this demand

00:33:41.320 --> 00:33:42.040
forecast model.

00:33:45.210 --> 00:33:49.000
The diagram shows that
the continuous deployment

00:33:49.000 --> 00:33:51.580
system in our client's case.

00:33:51.580 --> 00:33:56.170
First, we retrained the
model on a weekly basis.

00:33:56.170 --> 00:33:58.870
Due to the change
in the market, it

00:33:58.870 --> 00:34:01.660
is not possible to use
the same model more

00:34:01.660 --> 00:34:06.220
than [INAUDIBLE],, so we
introduced the retraining

00:34:06.220 --> 00:34:10.033
process on a regular basis.

00:34:10.033 --> 00:34:14.909
Sometimes the performance
model is under the threshold

00:34:14.909 --> 00:34:16.120
even after retraining.

00:34:16.120 --> 00:34:19.159
In such a situation,
Our ML engineers

00:34:19.159 --> 00:34:23.270
tried to modify the model itself
to keep the model performance

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:24.850
well.

00:34:24.850 --> 00:34:28.210
We [? developed ?] kind
of a monthly report,

00:34:28.210 --> 00:34:30.880
which is [? about ?]
model performance,

00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:34.630
so our client can understand
what we are doing.

00:34:34.630 --> 00:34:37.850
OK, this is the process of this
continuous [? deployment ?]

00:34:37.850 --> 00:34:40.449
[INAUDIBLE] help clients.

00:34:40.449 --> 00:34:43.112
And then [INAUDIBLE] details.

00:34:43.112 --> 00:34:46.840
[INAUDIBLE],, the
freshness of the data

00:34:46.840 --> 00:34:51.010
is very important to keep
the model performance well.

00:34:51.010 --> 00:34:54.070
In this client's case,
the [INAUDIBLE] data

00:34:54.070 --> 00:34:57.620
has a very strong
seasonality and is influenced

00:34:57.620 --> 00:35:01.350
by some other external events.

00:35:01.350 --> 00:35:03.670
So the retraining
with fresh data

00:35:03.670 --> 00:35:08.230
is a very good way to keep
up with these changes.

00:35:08.230 --> 00:35:12.070
It is also important to
monitor the model performance

00:35:12.070 --> 00:35:18.340
by comparing demand forecast and
actual result. In most cases,

00:35:18.340 --> 00:35:21.080
in my experience, this
retraining process

00:35:21.080 --> 00:35:22.625
works very well.

00:35:22.625 --> 00:35:26.330
So it would be a good idea
to automate [? just ?]

00:35:26.330 --> 00:35:27.820
repeatable processes.

00:35:27.820 --> 00:35:30.190
But sometimes it doesn't work.

00:35:33.501 --> 00:35:39.170
Even after training, the model
performance could be very bad.

00:35:39.170 --> 00:35:41.420
In such a situation,
ML engineers

00:35:41.420 --> 00:35:46.850
look into the original CRM
data and other data sources

00:35:46.850 --> 00:35:52.172
to find out what's
happening in real market.

00:35:52.172 --> 00:35:55.490
Typically this kind of a
performance degradation

00:35:55.490 --> 00:35:59.290
is caused by drastic
change in the input data.

00:35:59.290 --> 00:36:04.880
It is influenced by competitors'
promotions or a new product

00:36:04.880 --> 00:36:08.240
launch, and so on.

00:36:08.240 --> 00:36:12.830
After finding root causes
of this degradation,

00:36:12.830 --> 00:36:15.670
ML engineers think
about how they

00:36:15.670 --> 00:36:18.880
can modify the model future
to keep up with these changes.

00:36:21.530 --> 00:36:24.020
As you can imagine,
this investigation

00:36:24.020 --> 00:36:27.080
is very hard to standardize.

00:36:27.080 --> 00:36:30.110
But at least we are
building a dashboard

00:36:30.110 --> 00:36:38.310
to help ML engineers to work on
this investigation [INAUDIBLE]..

00:36:38.310 --> 00:36:42.740
Finally, let me talk
about risk management.

00:36:42.740 --> 00:36:45.120
One of [INAUDIBLE]
in this process

00:36:45.120 --> 00:36:50.510
is that there is no guarantee
that ML engineers can

00:36:50.510 --> 00:36:56.630
[INAUDIBLE] model performance
in a timely manner.

00:36:56.630 --> 00:37:00.070
There might be a data
collapse in our data pipeline,

00:37:00.070 --> 00:37:06.260
and the model cannot make
meaningful predictions for why.

00:37:06.260 --> 00:37:08.700
This is sort of a
nightmare scenario,

00:37:08.700 --> 00:37:13.920
and we should have a backup
plan for such a situation.

00:37:13.920 --> 00:37:17.480
In this client's case,
we defined a kind

00:37:17.480 --> 00:37:22.110
of a feedback plan with
clients in a very early stage

00:37:22.110 --> 00:37:24.380
of this project.

00:37:24.380 --> 00:37:28.820
If ML model doesn't
work at all, our clients

00:37:28.820 --> 00:37:31.160
switch back to the
manual operation.

00:37:31.160 --> 00:37:34.260
Of course, in this
operation there

00:37:34.260 --> 00:37:36.980
is no machine learning model.

00:37:36.980 --> 00:37:45.050
And then, so our clients
use demand forecast,

00:37:45.050 --> 00:37:49.640
which is from a human
planner, and then he

00:37:49.640 --> 00:37:55.760
used the statistical approach
to make this demand forecast.

00:37:55.760 --> 00:37:58.760
OK, this is what I want to talk.

00:37:58.760 --> 00:38:01.950
And then I'll talk.

00:38:06.940 --> 00:38:12.190
So I will note that the
customer case of this case

00:38:12.190 --> 00:38:13.550
are very simplified.

00:38:17.010 --> 00:38:22.980
Actually, in my project we have
a much more complex process

00:38:22.980 --> 00:38:24.760
and metrics.

00:38:24.760 --> 00:38:29.970
But anyway, the most important
thing in production environment

00:38:29.970 --> 00:38:37.130
is how quickly we can take this
kind of performance degradation

00:38:37.130 --> 00:38:40.800
and how quickly we can fix it.

00:38:40.800 --> 00:38:48.720
We are now building a general
platform to help ML engineers.

00:38:48.720 --> 00:38:51.000
Of course, it's
streamlining these kind

00:38:51.000 --> 00:38:58.280
of processes, including
monitoring and retraining

00:38:58.280 --> 00:39:01.540
[INAUDIBLE] model
and the deployment.

00:39:01.540 --> 00:39:04.520
So I will hope
this platform will

00:39:04.520 --> 00:39:09.020
be used in many machine learning
projects for our clients.

00:39:09.020 --> 00:39:09.830
Thank you.

00:39:09.830 --> 00:39:11.330
ETSUJI NAKAI: Thank
you, Kazusa-san.

00:39:11.330 --> 00:39:14.940
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:17.970
OK, so through his
real experience,

00:39:17.970 --> 00:39:21.270
I think you have some
realistic image what

00:39:21.270 --> 00:39:25.140
is a continuous deployment
in a real project.

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:28.590
so now, as a summary,
let me talk a little bit

00:39:28.590 --> 00:39:32.190
about the platform for
the continuous deployment.

00:39:32.190 --> 00:39:35.250
As Kazu-san told us,
he's now thinking

00:39:35.250 --> 00:39:38.580
about designing some
standardized platform,

00:39:38.580 --> 00:39:41.130
or general platform, for
the end-to-end machine

00:39:41.130 --> 00:39:45.540
learning, including the
continuous deployment process.

00:39:45.540 --> 00:39:48.770
And I think this is really
the frontier of the machine

00:39:48.770 --> 00:39:50.470
learning project.

00:39:50.470 --> 00:39:54.330
Some customers and partners
are realizing the importance

00:39:54.330 --> 00:39:57.510
of the continuous
deployment, and most of them

00:39:57.510 --> 00:40:00.220
are thinking about
what is best practice,

00:40:00.220 --> 00:40:04.380
what is the best platform,
to support that process?

00:40:04.380 --> 00:40:07.830
And we, Google, is the same.

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:12.225
Recently we released
a paper called "TFX."

00:40:12.225 --> 00:40:15.810
It is and end-to-end
machine learning platform

00:40:15.810 --> 00:40:18.810
used by Google in
[INAUDIBLE],, including

00:40:18.810 --> 00:40:21.000
the continuous deployment.

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:23.520
Some of our machine
learning services

00:40:23.520 --> 00:40:27.040
are actually learning
on this platform.

00:40:27.040 --> 00:40:29.580
Unfortunately, this
platform itself

00:40:29.580 --> 00:40:31.020
is not an external service.

00:40:31.020 --> 00:40:36.210
You cannot use this
platform as a GCP service.

00:40:36.210 --> 00:40:39.660
But at least the good news is
that some essential components

00:40:39.660 --> 00:40:45.190
used in this TFX is available
as a part of TensorFlow.

00:40:45.190 --> 00:40:48.270
You can see this
paper for details.

00:40:48.270 --> 00:40:51.870
So in theory you can
combine TensorFlow

00:40:51.870 --> 00:40:54.840
with some standard GCP
products and components

00:40:54.840 --> 00:40:59.390
to build your own continuous
deployment platform.

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:05.670
And for today's demonstration,
I combined TensorFlow

00:41:05.670 --> 00:41:08.910
with some standard
GCP components

00:41:08.910 --> 00:41:13.770
to build that
demonstration dashboard.

00:41:13.770 --> 00:41:16.730
However, please be careful.

00:41:16.730 --> 00:41:20.130
These specific components
are not important.

00:41:20.130 --> 00:41:23.400
These are just for the
sake of demonstration,

00:41:23.400 --> 00:41:27.600
especially depending on the
requirements of your project's

00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:30.030
functional or
non-functional requirements,

00:41:30.030 --> 00:41:32.650
these components
could be different.

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:34.930
The important thing
is to understand

00:41:34.930 --> 00:41:38.470
the general framework.

00:41:38.470 --> 00:41:42.720
And I think most end-to-end
machine learning platforms now

00:41:42.720 --> 00:41:47.550
have three components on the
bottom side of this chart.

00:41:47.550 --> 00:41:49.950
First, you need
some data platform

00:41:49.950 --> 00:41:53.850
to collect the trend data from
the business data pipeline.

00:41:53.850 --> 00:41:57.030
And then you have some training
platform, like Cloud Machine

00:41:57.030 --> 00:42:00.840
Learning Engine, to train your
model with these training data.

00:42:00.840 --> 00:42:04.230
And finally, you have
some serving platform

00:42:04.230 --> 00:42:06.690
to deploy the training
machine learning model

00:42:06.690 --> 00:42:08.640
to the real world.

00:42:08.640 --> 00:42:12.720
However, to [? realize ?] the
continuous deployment practice,

00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:15.090
you need one
additional component--

00:42:15.090 --> 00:42:19.590
that is a validation
platform on the top side.

00:42:19.590 --> 00:42:21.780
With this validation
platform, you

00:42:21.780 --> 00:42:25.550
keep monitoring the accuracy
of your machine learning model.

00:42:25.550 --> 00:42:30.470
And ideally, this platform
has some visualization too.

00:42:30.470 --> 00:42:32.460
With these
visualizations, you can

00:42:32.460 --> 00:42:35.800
understand the trend of the
accuracy of your machine

00:42:35.800 --> 00:42:38.280
learning model and
then figure out

00:42:38.280 --> 00:42:41.410
the best timing to retrain
your machine learning model

00:42:41.410 --> 00:42:43.760
or redesign your
machine learning

00:42:43.760 --> 00:42:47.120
model by [? hiring ?]
data scientists again.

00:42:47.120 --> 00:42:51.420
And also, it should have
some automation mechanism

00:42:51.420 --> 00:42:53.580
like today's demonstration.

00:42:53.580 --> 00:42:56.550
Just with a single click,
you can retrain your machine

00:42:56.550 --> 00:42:57.210
learning model.

00:42:57.210 --> 00:42:59.070
With just a single
click, you can

00:42:59.070 --> 00:43:03.940
redeploy your machine learning
model to the real world.

00:43:03.940 --> 00:43:07.710
So this is a sort
of call for action.

00:43:07.710 --> 00:43:11.030
If you're now working on some
realistic real machine learning

00:43:11.030 --> 00:43:15.210
project and want to apply
the continuous deployment

00:43:15.210 --> 00:43:17.350
in your project,
please let's start

00:43:17.350 --> 00:43:20.310
to have a discussion
with your team members

00:43:20.310 --> 00:43:24.000
to think about what is
the most important feature

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:26.760
in your continuous
deployment platform.

00:43:26.760 --> 00:43:32.160
What is the best monitoring
dashboard for your project?

00:43:32.160 --> 00:43:36.600
OK, in that sense, I want to
ask one additional question

00:43:36.600 --> 00:43:37.650
to Kazu-san.

00:43:37.650 --> 00:43:39.619
Kazu-san, are you still here?

00:43:39.619 --> 00:43:40.910
TAKETOSHI KAZUSA: Hi, I'm here.

00:43:40.910 --> 00:43:42.160
ETSUJI NAKAI: Thank you.

00:43:42.160 --> 00:43:45.240
OK, my question
is that he told us

00:43:45.240 --> 00:43:51.150
that now he's planning to
design his own machine learning

00:43:51.150 --> 00:43:54.300
platform, including the
continuous deployment

00:43:54.300 --> 00:43:58.060
for customers of his company.

00:43:58.060 --> 00:44:02.700
So what do you think is
the most important feature

00:44:02.700 --> 00:44:07.046
of the machine learning
platform for your customers.

00:44:07.046 --> 00:44:09.680
TAKETOSHI KAZUSA:
OK, that question

00:44:09.680 --> 00:44:12.750
is a little bit
complex for me, so I'd

00:44:12.750 --> 00:44:15.842
like to answer this
question in Japanese.

00:44:15.842 --> 00:44:17.050
ETSUJI NAKAI: OK, no problem.

00:44:17.050 --> 00:44:17.790
I will translate.

00:44:20.700 --> 00:44:59.810
TAKETOSHI KAZUSA:
[SPEAKING JAPANESE]

00:44:59.810 --> 00:45:01.440
Sorry about my Japanese.

00:45:01.440 --> 00:45:03.840
ETSUJI NAKAI: OK,
that's no problem.

00:45:03.840 --> 00:45:08.750
I think he talked a
really important topic.

00:45:08.750 --> 00:45:13.650
So it's really my challenge
to translate it accurately.

00:45:13.650 --> 00:45:17.720
OK, basically he told me
that the most important thing

00:45:17.720 --> 00:45:21.030
in machine learning
project is to evaluate

00:45:21.030 --> 00:45:26.790
the performance of the model in
a real business context using

00:45:26.790 --> 00:45:28.920
the real business data.

00:45:28.920 --> 00:45:31.420
It means in today's
demonstration,

00:45:31.420 --> 00:45:36.030
we simply tested the trained
model against the test set.

00:45:36.030 --> 00:45:40.500
But in his project, as he
talked in his presentation,

00:45:40.500 --> 00:45:45.270
he compared the
actual business output

00:45:45.270 --> 00:45:50.230
against the model's predictions.

00:45:50.230 --> 00:45:56.070
So for that, he needs to combine
the business data pipeline

00:45:56.070 --> 00:45:59.010
with the machine
learning pipeline,

00:45:59.010 --> 00:46:04.380
because he needs very fresh
business outcome data.

00:46:04.380 --> 00:46:10.230
It doesn't make any sense if he
evaluates the performance weeks

00:46:10.230 --> 00:46:12.270
or months later.

00:46:12.270 --> 00:46:17.010
Ideally, he must evaluate
the result in real time

00:46:17.010 --> 00:46:19.600
with real-time business data.

00:46:19.600 --> 00:46:22.230
So the challenge
is how to combine

00:46:22.230 --> 00:46:25.690
these two platforms, business
platform and machine learning

00:46:25.690 --> 00:46:26.730
platform.

00:46:26.730 --> 00:46:30.765
So his idea does he
wants his platform

00:46:30.765 --> 00:46:35.880
to be capable of integrating
with business data

00:46:35.880 --> 00:46:39.130
pipeline with a machine
learning pipeline.

00:46:39.130 --> 00:46:41.490
I think now it is a very
common understanding

00:46:41.490 --> 00:46:45.990
that synchronizing business
data with machine learning

00:46:45.990 --> 00:46:50.460
to collect our training
data in a timely manner

00:46:50.460 --> 00:46:52.260
is very important.

00:46:52.260 --> 00:46:56.790
But he told me that it is
also important to synchronize

00:46:56.790 --> 00:47:03.960
the evaluation data from
the business data platform.

00:47:03.960 --> 00:47:05.860
OK, that's really a good lesson.

00:47:05.860 --> 00:47:07.110
Thank you very much, Kazu-san.

00:47:07.110 --> 00:47:08.693
TAKETOSHI KAZUSA:
Thank you very much.

00:47:08.693 --> 00:47:11.558
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:12.530 --> 00:47:13.530
ETSUJI NAKAI: OK.

00:47:13.530 --> 00:47:14.490
Yeah, it's surprising.

00:47:14.490 --> 00:47:17.550
I really learned a new
thing in this presentation

00:47:17.550 --> 00:47:18.810
from Kazu-san.

00:47:18.810 --> 00:47:23.550
And I think it is really
useful for your project too.

00:47:23.550 --> 00:47:26.670
And unfortunately, I
don't have much time

00:47:26.670 --> 00:47:32.050
to talk about details, more
details, in a Q&amp;A session.

00:47:32.050 --> 00:47:33.960
But basically, I'm
really happy to have

00:47:33.960 --> 00:47:38.290
further discussion in person
with you if you really like.

